# Concurrent item processing in Go — checklist

**Own every goroutine.** Never fire one off without a way to know it finished. Either `errgroup.Group` (from `golang.org/x/sync/errgroup`) when you want the first error and shared cancellation, or `sync.WaitGroup` when you just need "all done". Go 1.25 added `WaitGroup.Go`; use it only if the module's `go` directive targets >= 1.25.

```go
g, ctx := errgroup.WithContext(ctx)
for _, item := range items {
    g.Go(func() error {
        return process(ctx, item)
    })
}
if err := g.Wait(); err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("processing items: %w", err)
}
```

**Watch the loop variable.** Pre-Go 1.22, `for _, item := range items { go func() { use(item) }() }` captured the same variable. Go 1.22+ scopes per-iteration, but if `go.mod` says `go 1.21` or older, pass `item` as a parameter or shadow it.

**Bound the fan-out.** One goroutine per item is fine for small batches; for unbounded input use a worker pool or a semaphore (`golang.org/x/sync/semaphore`) so you don't spawn 100k goroutines or hammer a downstream service.

**Propagate `context.Context`.** Accept it as the first parameter of `process` and forward it. That gives you cooperative cancellation when one worker fails (`errgroup.WithContext` cancels `ctx` for the rest) and deadlines/timeouts. Never stash `ctx` in a struct.

**Guard shared state.** Concurrent writes to a `map`, slice append races, shared counters — use a mutex, `sync.Map`, or an atomic. Better: have workers send results over a channel to a single collector goroutine so no locking is needed.

**Collect errors, don't swallow them.** `errgroup` returns the first error. If you need *all* errors, collect them yourself (with a mutex or via a channel) — but decide up front whether you want fail-fast or best-effort.

**Always wait before returning.** A function that starts goroutines and returns without `g.Wait()` will race with its caller and with process shutdown — the classic goroutine leak.

**Run `go test -race`.** The race detector catches most of the above before review does.

